{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in collab/1790749088000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md collab/1790749088000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-r2.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob.gpt-6-sol.findings.md collab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob.glm-5.3.findings.md; do echo \"$f\"; nl -ba \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"collab/1790749088000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md\n     1\tCoding-Agent: agy\n     2\tSession-ID: c1020f8c-3056-4129-a680-cdc1337b844a\n     3\tModel: gemini-3.1-pro-high\n     4\t\n     5\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-30 06:20:29 GMT\n     6\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 13:20:29 +0700\n     7\t\n     8\tHere is the architectural design requested:\n     9\t\n    10\t1. Fragmentation/reassembly (DRDS or other):\n    11\t- **Implementation gap**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:78-81` (`send-datagram!` throws if payload exceeds `max-datagram`), confirmed by `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:157` (\"Anything beyond the 1200-byte datagram budget is refused\").\n    12\t- **Where it lives**: `dao.jing.dht.node` should transition from Transit-JSON to the canonical CBOR codec, reusing the `fragment-envelope` already defined in `src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151` (`fragment-envelope`).\n    13\t- **Integrity**: Per-fragment hashes are unnecessary. Hash verification occurs strictly on the reassembled whole in `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:182` (`accept-bytes`).\n    14\t- **Limits & Loss/Retry**: Receiver enforces `max-message-bytes` during reassembly (`udp.cljc:332`). Missing fragments cause the reassembly to time out; the client owns the request timeout and retries the entire segment.\n    15\t\n    16\t2. Replication lifecycle:\n    17\t- **Architectural defect**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:216` (`make-put` uses `run! deref (mapv #(future ...) ...)`, blocking the caller and occupying JVM executors until all peer attempts finish).\n    18\t- **Implementation gap**: Asynchronous outbox.\n    19\t- **Contract**: An acknowledgement from `put` means \"durably stored locally and queued for replication\".\n    20\t- **State**: The DHT handle `create-content-dht` (`dht.cljc:312`) must embed an explicit `:outbox` (a `dao.stream.memory-log`) and a runner state, with no hidden callbacks.\n    21\t- **Blocking avoidance**: A REPL round appends the target address to the outbox and immediately receives `:inserted`, allowing the round to proceed while the runner drains the outbox to the network.\n    22\t\n    23\t3. Non-JVM hosts:\n    24\t- **Implementation gap**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:14` states \"JVM only today (java.net.DatagramSocket); no browser/Node peer\".\n    25\t- **Design**: Direct UDP transport implementing `IDhtNet` must be written for CLJD (via `dart:io` `RawDatagramSocket`) and Node/CLJS (via `dgram`).\n    26\t- **Deferred work**: Browser CLJS support. Browsers cannot use raw UDP and will require a `dao.stream.remote` relay to satisfy `IDhtNet`, consistent with the P2P no-privileged-node invariant.\n    27\t\n    28\t4. Hardening:\n    29\t- **Architectural defect / Implementation gap**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:22` (\"No address validation or rate limiting yet\") and `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:161` (no returnability validation).\n    30\t- **Required before on-by-default**: Returnability cookies (a challenge-response mechanism to verify the `:from` IP before accepting large `store-content` payloads) to prevent UDP amplification attacks. Token-bucket rate limiting per peer must be added to the receiver loop (`node.cljc:144`).\n    31\t\n    32\t5. Latest-root discovery:\n    33\t- **Architectural rule**: `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:11` explicitly dictates \"The DHT answers one question: given a content address, where on the network are the bytes? It does nothing else. There are no roots...\".\n    34\t- **Deferred work**: Root discovery strictly belongs OUTSIDE the DHT. A reader learns the latest manifest address via a `dao.stream` of root facts over `dao.stream.remote` rendezvous, not through mutable Kademlia records.\n    35\t\n    36\t6. Bootstrap/config:\n    37\t- **Implementation gap**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:304` (`:bootstrap` seq exists, but relies on direct IP reachability).\n    38\t- **Deferred work**: NAT traversal, hole punching, and relay (specified in `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md`).\n    39\t- **Offline behaviour**: A \"local-only DHT\" is instantiated with an empty `:bootstrap` list. It routes exclusively to its `dao.jing.file` or `mem` local backend and queues or drops outbound replication.\n    40\t\n    41\t7. Integration with yin.repl:\n    42\t- **Implementation gap**: `src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:148` (`make-indexer`) currently expects a generic `content-store`. The startup specification mechanism (`1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:10`) must be extended to accept `{:type :dht :dir ... :bootstrap [...]}` or a `dht:<dir>` string.\n    43\t- **Published**: The covered datom index and B-tree nodes are appended to the intake stream and materialized by the DHT's local store, which then asynchronously replicates them.\n    44\t- **Query entry point**: `dao.space.query/q` (`docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md:55`) resolves nodes. A local miss cascades to the `IDhtNet` grid fetch, which verifies the payload and caches it locally.\n    45\t\n    46\t8. Slicing:\n    47\t- **Slice 1**: CBOR codec transition and DRDS fragmentation in `dao.jing.dht.node`.\n    48\t- **Slice 2**: Asynchronous outbox replication in `dao.jing.dht`.\n    49\t- **Slice 3**: Non-JVM UDP node implementations (CLJD, Node/CLJS).\n    50\t- **Slice 4**: Returnability hardening and rate limits.\n    51\t- **Slice 5**: `yin.repl` startup integration (adding the `:dht` store spec).\n    52\t- **Concurrency**: Slices 1-4 do not touch `yin/repl/*` and can run concurrently with the in-flight `yin.repl` work. Slice 5 is the dependency integration.\n    53\t\n    54\t9. OWNER decisions:\n    55\t- **Default store behaviour**: Default remains `mem`. `dht:` must be explicitly requested.\n    56\t- **Network on/off**: Network is OFF by default (local-only mode, empty bootstrap) to prevent unwanted broadcast/listening.\n    57\t- **Access policy**: The DHT is an open network. No sensitive REPL data should be published until ShiBi authorization exists.\n    58\t\n    59\tI have also captured this architecture design in an implementation plan artifact for your review as per the `/plan` instruction. Let me know if you would like me to proceed with implementing any of the slices!\n    60\tThe task outlined in `collab/1790749088000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic.prompt.md` is explicitly defined as a read-only architectural design task:\n    61\t\n    62\t> \"Perform a read-only architecture design. Do not edit files. You are headless; your final response is the deliverable.\"\n    63\t\n    64\tAs such, the \"execution\" of this specific prompt is complete with the delivery of the architecture design and the implementation plan artifact. \n    65\t\n    66\tIf you would like me to override the \"Do not edit files\" directive from the prompt and proceed with implementing the slices (e.g., starting with Slice 1: Codec & Fragmentation), please let me know! Otherwise, this architectural pass is fully complete.\n    67\t\ncollab/1790749088000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-r2.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md\n     1\tCoding-Agent: agy\n     2\tSession-ID: c1020f8c-3056-4129-a680-cdc1337b844a\n     3\tModel: gemini-3.1-pro-high\n     4\t\n     5\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-30 06:40:49 GMT\n     6\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 13:40:49 +0700\n     7\t\n     8\t1. **Outbox durability contradiction**: \n     9\tReplication is explicitly best-effort-after-ack with loss on crash. The outbox is an ephemeral `dao.stream.memory-log`.\n    10\t- *Ack contract*: \"Durably stored locally; queued for best-effort replication. The replication queue is ephemeral and lost on process crash.\"\n    11\t- *Runner*: Driven by an explicit `(step! runner)` function invoked by the host's event loop (no hidden threads or callbacks). The runner reads the memory-log, performs `dht/lookup`, and sends `store-content!`.\n    12\t- *Empty bootstrap*: The runner explicitly **drops** the replication request if there are no known peers (`k`=0), avoiding memory leaks in local-only mode.\n    13\t\n    14\t2. **Slices 1-5**:\n    15\t- **Slice 1: CBOR Codec & DRDS Fragmentation**\n    16\t  - *Files*: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc`.\n    17\t  - *API Changes*: None to `create-node`.\n    18\t  - *Wire Format*: Transition from Transit-JSON to a CBOR map `{:op op :rpc id :from peer :address a :v bytes}`. Fragmentation uses `dao.stream.udp/fragment-envelope` (`{:dao.stream.remote/id ... :dao.stream.udp/part ...}`). Payload limit: `64KB`.\n    19\t  - *Acceptance Tests*:\n    20\t    - Input < 1200 bytes -> sent as single datagram, successfully received/decoded.\n    21\t    - Input > 1200 bytes -> fragmented, reassembled, hash-verified, stored.\n    22\t    - Lost fragment -> receiver reassembly times out/drops, sender request times out.\n    23\t    - Oversize input (> 64KB) -> throws locally on send, receiver drops if declared parts exceed bound.\n    24\t    - Hash mismatch -> reassembled payload fails `accept-bytes`, dropped.\n    25\t  - *Host Lanes*: CLJ.\n    26\t- **Slice 2: Async Outbox Replication**\n    27\t  - *Files*: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc`.\n    28\t  - *API Changes*: `create-content-dht` returns a handle containing `:outbox` (a `memory-log`). Added `(create-runner dht-handle)` and `(step! runner)`.\n    29\t  - *Wire Format*: Unchanged.\n    30\t  - *Acceptance Tests*:\n    31\t    - `put` immediately returns `:inserted` without blocking.\n    32\t    - Calling `step!` pulls from `:outbox` and calls `store-content!` to peers.\n    33\t    - Crash/restart (simulated by creating a new DHT handle with the same local store) -> pending outbox is lost, local durability intact.\n    34\t  - *Host Lanes*: CLJ.\n    35\t- **Slice 3: Non-JVM UDP Node Implementations**\n    36\t  - *Files*: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc`.\n    37\t  - *API Changes*: None.\n    38\t  - *Wire Format*: Unchanged.\n    39\t  - *Acceptance Tests*:\n    40\t    - End-to-end `ping` and `store-content!` exchange over UDP.\n    41\t  - *Host Lanes*: CLJ, CLJS (Node `dgram`), CLJD (`dart:io` `RawDatagramSocket`).\n    42\t- **Slice 4: Returnability Hardening**\n    43\t  - *Files*: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc`.\n    44\t  - *API Changes*: None.\n    45\t  - *Wire Format*: New op `:cookie-challenge` with `:cookie` field. Client adds `:cookie` to retried requests.\n    46\t  - *Acceptance Tests*:\n    47\t    - Unverified peer sends large `:store-content` -> receiver replies `:cookie-challenge`, sender retries with `:cookie`, receiver accepts.\n    48\t    - Rate-limit hit -> receiver drops request.\n    49\t    - Returnability failure (spoofed IP) -> attacker never receives cookie, request dropped.\n    50\t  - *Host Lanes*: CLJ, CLJS, CLJD.\n    51\t- **Slice 5: yin.repl Integration**\n    52\t  - *Files*: `src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/repl.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc`.\n    53\t  - *API Changes*: `index-store-spec` accepts `{:type :dht :dir ... :bootstrap [...]}`.\n    54\t  - *Wire Format*: Unchanged.\n    55\t  - *Acceptance Tests*:\n    56\t    - Start REPL with DHT spec, evaluate program, verify `dao.space.query/q` resolves facts locally.\n    57\t    - Second REPL fetches B-tree nodes from first via DHT grid.\n    58\t  - *Host Lanes*: CLJ, CLJS, CLJD.\n    59\t\n    60\t3. **Returnability cookies**:\n    61\t- *Exchange*:\n    62\t  1. Receiver gets `:store-content` or `:find-node` from an unverified IP.\n    63\t  2. Receiver drops the request payload and replies with `{:op :cookie-challenge, :cookie HMAC(IP + secret)}` (stateless HMAC, no cache needed).\n    64\t  3. Sender receives `:cookie-challenge` and retries the original request, including `:cookie HMAC`.\n    65\t  4. Receiver verifies the HMAC. If valid, processes the request and caches the verified IP in a short-lived LRU cache (e.g., 5-minute expiry) to skip challenging subsequent requests from that peer.\n    66\t- *Required Ops*: `:store-content` (large incoming payload), `:find-node` (amplification vector).\n    67\t\n    68\t4. **Concurrency with in-flight work**:\n    69\t- Slices 1-4 touch ZERO files in `yin/repl/*`.\n    70\t- *Files touched per slice*:\n    71\t  - **Slice 1**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc`.\n    72\t  - **Slice 2**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc`.\n    73\t  - **Slice 3**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc`.\n    74\t  - **Slice 4**: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc`.\n    75\t- *Parallelism*: Slice 2 (touches `dht.cljc`) can run in parallel with Slices 1, 3, and 4 (which touch `node.cljc`). Slices 1, 3, and 4 modify the exact same transport layer file and should be sequenced (Slice 1 -> Slice 3 -> Slice 4) to avoid merge conflicts on `node.cljc`.\n    76\t\n    77\t5. **Verify, don't assume**:\n    78\t- `fragment-envelope` in `dao.stream.udp`: `src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151` (`(defn- fragment-envelope [id part parts direction chunk] ...)`)\n    79\t- `accept-bytes`: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:167` (`(defn- accept-bytes [address b64] ...)`)\n    80\t- `make-put`: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:203` (`(defn- make-put [{:keys [net local], :as handle}] ...)`)\n    81\t- the node receive loop: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:144` (`(defn- start-receiver! ...)`)\n    82\t\n    83\t*(Note: The plan artifact has also been updated with this round 2 architecture design).*\n    84\tThe read-only architectural design task for Round 2 is fully complete as requested. \n    85\t\n    86\tThe prompt explicitly states:\n    87\t> \"Read-only: do not edit or create any files; do not implement anything. (the orchestrator dispatches implementation separately).\"\n    88\t\n    89\tTherefore, I will pause here and not proceed with implementing the slices to honor the strict read-only directive. If you need me to override this and begin implementing the slices anyway, or if there's anything else you'd like me to look at, just let me know!\n    90\t\ncollab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n     1\tCoding-Agent: codex\n     2\tSession-ID: 01a0f10a-6dcd-7af1-b670-78f74255321a\n     3\tModel: gpt-6-sol\n     4\t\n     5\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-30 06:41:01 GMT\n     6\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 13:41:01 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     7\t\n     8\t## A. Critique of slices 1–5\n     9\t\n    10\t1. **Fragmentation: necessary, but the proposed slice crosses a protocol boundary.** The current DHT refuses requests above 1200 bytes and drops oversized replies, so large index nodes cannot reliably leave the local store ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:74), [node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:186)). The existing `dao.stream.udp` framing is defined for remote-stream request IDs and directions, with a 64 KiB default message limit ([udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151), [udp.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:230)). **I disagree** that the DHT node can simply reuse that private envelope or that moving its Transit-JSON wire to CBOR is required. Either run DHT messages over a specified stream channel, or extract a transport-neutral fragmenter with its own message identity and bounds. Preserve the current wire during an independent framing change unless a versioned migration is justified. Whole-content hash checking already protects accepted bytes ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:167)); per-fragment hashes are optional, but reassembly limits, source scoping, expiry or eviction, and retries are required.\n    11\t\n    12\t2. **Replication: the blocking put is real; the proposed acknowledgement is unsound.** A put waits for peer attempts after its local write ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:203)); each request can take three 500 ms attempts ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:231)). **I disagree** with “durably stored locally and queued” when the queue is `dao.stream.memory-log`: that log explicitly lasts only for the process ([memory_log.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:1)). Choose one honest contract before coding: local durability with *volatile, best-effort* replication, or local durability plus a separately durable replication intent recorded before acknowledgement. An empty bootstrap must retain pending intent for later peer discovery under the latter contract; silently dropping it contradicts “queued.” Define bounded backlog, retry, shutdown, and recovery behavior. The local backend must be `dao.jing.file` for a durable claim; the DHT constructor currently accepts memory as local storage ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:285)).\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Non-JVM support: transport choice precedes host ports.** The author’s separate CLJD and Node UDP implementations are possible, but **I disagree** that they are the preferred architectural slice. The owner’s invariant makes any stream exposable through WebSocket or UDP, with NAT traversal and no privileged server role ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:13)). A DHT `IDhtNet` adapter over symmetric `dao.stream.remote` channels fits that boundary better than three independent socket protocol stacks. Reuse the existing UDP channel where applicable; use WebSocket or relay paths where raw UDP is unavailable. A browser cannot listen or use raw UDP ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:32)). Preserve `IDhtNet` as a useful seam ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:53)), but specify peer identity, reply correlation, deadlines, and channel loss through stream events, consistent with the six invariants ([datom.world.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md:24)).\n    15\t\n    16\t4. **Hardening: correct concern, incomplete mechanism.** The receiver learns claimed `:from` peers before validating returnability and processes requests on its receiving thread ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:144)). A cookie must bind the observed source address and port, a short validity period, and a server-held secret; issue it in a small challenge reply and require it before expensive lookup responses, content reads, or store processing. Check reply size against request size until validation; cap bytes, work, fragments, pending RPCs, and storage per source and globally. A per-peer token bucket keyed only by claimed `:from` is spoofable. Also match replies to the expected source, not merely the RPC number ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:176)). This is an architectural threat-model decision followed by implementation work.\n    17\t\n    18\t5. **REPL integration: premature as slice 5.** The DHT has no roots by design ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:9)), while the durable REPL design needs a validated published HEAD and recovery path ([durable-store findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:15)). **I disagree** that a `:dht` startup spec and content fetch alone produce a usable cross-process index. A reader needs a trusted current manifest address, bootstrap reachability, and a complete fetchable index. The proposed plan also omits a slice for root discovery, availability repair, and storage limits. The existing lookup can strand live peers behind dead closest peers ([dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:107)); best-effort replication plus unbounded storage does not establish persistent network availability ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:169)).\n    19\t\n    20\tThe DHT document’s “sha256 only” statement is stale: the implemented store accepts registered segment algorithms ([dao.jing.dht.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:17), [dht.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:299)); Jing’s registry includes BLAKE3 and SHA-256, with BLAKE3 the default ([jing.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:207)).\n    21\t\n    22\t## B. Owner-policy recommendations\n    23\t\n    24\t1. **Keep `mem` as the default; require explicit `dht:`.** That preserves the agreed startup contract and prevents a change in latency, durability, network exposure, and failure modes merely by upgrading the REPL ([durable-store findings](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:7)). A `dht:` choice must name a durable local directory if it promises persistence.\n    25\t\n    26\t2. **Keep networking off until explicitly configured.** Empty bootstrap alone is insufficient: the current UDP constructor still binds a socket and starts a receiver ([node.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc:307)). Define local-only as *no listener, outbound dial, or replication worker*; enabling networking requires an explicit peer or meeting configuration. Once enabled, bootstrap addresses are ordinary peers, never authorities. NAT failure remains an observable connectivity state, not silent success ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:32)).\n    27\t\n    28\t3. **Do not publish sensitive REPL content to an open DHT before authorization exists.** “Sensitive” means anything the REPL intake can contain that the user has not explicitly marked public: source text, literals and secrets, AST or code rows, execution state, metadata, and index nodes or manifests that reveal those facts. Jing is deliberately payload-agnostic, so it cannot infer sensitivity ([dao.jing.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.jing.md:30)). Enforce the policy at REPL composition: reject network-enabled `dht:` publication by default; allow only an explicit public-data opt-in for that index, with a clear statement that peers can retain and redistribute every published segment. A warning alone does not enforce it. Encryption without key and access policy would still expose addresses and traffic metadata ([dao.stream.remote.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:45)).\n    29\t\n    30\t## C. Decisions before implementation\n    31\t\n    32\tDecide now: the acknowledgement and outbox durability contract; whether DHT traffic uses `dao.stream.remote` or a standalone versioned wire; maximum segment and reassembly size; the security boundary and public opt-in; what “local-only” opens; root-fact authority and conflict rule; and the availability promise (including what a peer acknowledgement proves). These shape interfaces and acceptance tests.\n    33\t\n    34\tPer slice, choose concrete codec, fragment sizing, retry timings, token-bucket values, host adapters, and CLI syntax. Root discovery can remain outside the DHT without a privileged node: a writer appends signed `{writer-id, sequence, previous-root, manifest-address}` facts to an explicitly configured durable `dao.stream`; peers expose or relay that stream via `dao.stream.remote`. A reader obtains a descriptor through configured peers or a meeting stream, verifies the trusted writer key and monotonic chain, then fetches the manifest by address. The stream’s location is bootstrap information; competing writers require an explicit application policy, not DHT consensus.\n    35\t\n    36\t## D. Recommended slice plan\n    37\t\n    38\t| Order | Slice and acceptance criterion |\n    39\t|---|---|\n    40\t| 1 | **Contract and local mode.** Specify the decisions in C, correct the stale hash documentation, and expose DHT local-only with a file backend. Restart retains acknowledged content; no socket opens without explicit network configuration. |\n    41\t| 2 | **Transport and large messages.** Implement the selected stream-channel `IDhtNet` path and bounded fragmentation. Cross-host put/fetch of manifest and index-node-sized payloads succeeds under loss and reordering; oversize input fails explicitly; hash mismatch never enters the local store. |\n    42\t| 3 | **Asynchronous replication.** Implement the chosen durable or explicitly volatile intent contract. Put returns after its stated local guarantee; empty-bootstrap intents behave as specified; restart, retries, backpressure, and close preserve that contract. |\n    43\t| 4 | **Security and availability.** Add returnability, source-bound reply validation, resource limits, dead-peer replacement, and repair or republishing policy. Spoofed and oversized traffic stays within stated bounds; a live next-nearest peer can supply content when nearer peers fail. |\n    44\t| 5 | **Root-fact stream.** Publish and read signed monotonic root facts over a configured remote stream. A fresh reader given only trust key and bootstrap descriptor finds the latest accepted manifest; stale, forged, and conflicting facts have specified outcomes. |\n    45\t| 6 | **`yin.repl` integration.** After the in-flight durable-store work lands, add explicit `dht:` startup selection, public-data gate, recovery, and read-only cross-process query. A second process discovers the root, fetches and verifies the full covered index, and queries prior code; offline restart uses the local durable copy. |\n    46\t\n    47\tSlices 2 and 3 can proceed concurrently after slice 1 if their transport and intent interfaces are fixed. Slice 4 can develop alongside them against those interfaces; slice 5 can proceed independently of REPL work. **No slice before 6 touches `yin/repl/*`.** The current round publishes blobs, drains intake, and reads its manifest back ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:232)); slice 6 must preserve that local publication guarantee while reporting network publication as a separate, explicit state.\ncollab/1790750376000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic-mob.glm-5.3.findings.md\n     1\tCoding-Agent: glm\n     2\tSession-ID: 305a156d-0f83-4b26-8ed3-600af4c94594\n     3\tModel: glm-5.3\n     4\t\n     5\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-30 06:47:00 GMT\n     6\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-30 13:47:00 +07 (+0700)\n     7\t\n     8\tIndependent critique of `collab/1790749088000-architect-dao-jing-dht-epic.gemini-3.1-pro-high.findings.md` (the \"author\" below). All file:line references verified against the working tree at HEAD `48ec9d66`.\n     9\t\n    10\t## Ground truth verified first\n    11\t\n    12\t1. **The stale doc line is stale.** `docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md:19` (\"routes only `:segment/sha256-<64 hex>`\") contradicts the code: `src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:299-301` (docstring: \"registered `:segment/<algorithm>-...`\"), `content-target` at `dht.cljc:38-46` accepts any `jing/segment-address?`, and `node.cljc:117` likewise. The registry carries `:blake3` (default) and `:sha256` (`docs/design/dao.jing.md:224-232`), and the code index mints blake3. Fix is a doc edit; fold into slice 1.\n    13\t2. **The five blockers are real.** Datagram refusal on send (`node.cljc:76-80`) and dropped oversized replies (`node.cljc:190-195`); JVM-only (`node.cljc:13-17`); blocking put (`dht.cljc:216-223` — `run! deref` over futures, admitted by `dao.jing.dht.md:74-81,175-179`); no returnability/rate limiting (`node.cljc:22-25`, `dao.jing.dht.md:161-164`); no roots by design (`dao.jing.dht.md:11-15`).\n    14\t3. **Two facts the author missed.** `dao.jing.content` replaced `dao.jing.remote` and is already **committed** (`8894d81f`), so \"a `dao.stream.remote` relay\" is no longer future work on the content-service half. And the durable-store startup spec mechanism the author's slice 5 extends **does not exist yet** — no `--index-store`/`:index-store-spec` in `src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc`; `repl.cljc:813` still takes a handle defaulting to mem. Slice 5 is gated on another epic, not merely \"the integration slice.\"\n    15\t4. `docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md` is proposal-only (\"Nothing here is implemented\"), which constrains the root-discovery answer below.\n    16\t\n    17\t## A. Ruling on the author's slices 1–5\n    18\t\n    19\t**Slice 1 (CBOR + DRDS in `dao.jing.dht.node`) — right problem, wrong home.** Architectural defect in the design, not just a gap: as specified it grows a *second* fragmentation implementation beside the one that already exists and is cross-host. `fragment-envelope` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:151-157`) is private, and the machinery around it — chunk sizing (`udp.cljc:168-195`), oversize refusal (`udp.cljc:225-233`), bounded reassembly (`udp.cljc:316-361`), source-address-only replies (`udp.cljc:373-399`) — is only reusable for free if the DHT *rides* `dao.stream.udp` as its datagram layer. My ruling on the fork the brief names (weak point 5): **implement `IDhtNet` over a `dao.stream.udp` port** (`make-port` + `send-to!`, `udp.cljc:202-261`), with per-host socket seams feeding `receive!` — exactly the adapter contract its nsdoc states. This satisfies stream primacy (axiom 1, `docs/design/datom.world.md:16`; owner invariant), reuses the 1200-byte figure the DHT itself contributed (`udp.cljc:58-62`), makes slice 3 two thin seams instead of three peers, and makes the browser a channel swap (ws §3.1 / pair relay §3.3) instead of a new transport. The full mirror/link machinery of `dao.stream.remote` §2 is *not* needed — the DHT keeps its own rpc correlation and client-owned retries; only the channel (§3.2) is used.\n    20\t\n    21\t**On the CBOR switch (weak point 3): justified, but by the transport decision, not by fragmentation.** `dao.stream.transit` is portable (clj/cljs/cljd branches, `transit.cljc:6-10`), so portability does not force it; but §3.2 pins \"one CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile,\" and the pinned canonical codec (`dao.jing.cbor`) is the profile every Jing boundary already shares. Scope: wire only — `IDhtNet` (`dht.cljc:53-86`) and the store handle unchanged; Base64-in-application-value stays per `dao.jing.cbor.md:464-471`; the clean break with no negotiation (`dao.jing.cbor.md:496-503`) is acceptable only because no fleet exists — it must be slice 1 or never.\n    22\t\n    23\t**Integrity/limits:** agree per-fragment hashes are unnecessary — whole-segment verification is `accept-bytes` (`dht.cljc:167-180`) plus ingress re-verify (`node.cljc:114-127`). One consequence the author does not draw: fragmentation *creates* the amplification vector. Today's worst case is ~1200 bytes of reply for ~100 bytes of request (~12×); after slice 1 it is 64 KiB across ~60 datagrams (~60×). See slice 4.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Slice 2 (async outbox) — defect correctly identified; contract as written is contradictory (weak point 1).** `dao.stream.memory-log` is process-lifetime by its own docstring: \"Process-lifetime, not durable across restart… Durability is dao.jing's job, not this transport's\" (`memory_log.cljc:3-6`). So \"an acknowledgement means *durably stored locally and queued*\" is half-false: the local half is durable only if `:local` is `dao.jing.file`, and the queue half is never durable. Resolution: **weaken the contract, do not durable-ify the outbox.** The local store already *is* the durable record of what exists; because replication is content-addressed and idempotent (`dao.jing.dht.md:83-86`), replication intent is re-derivable from it, and a restart-time re-enqueue sweep can recover lost intent. The honest sentence: *the acknowledgement is the local `:inserted`/`:present` verdict; replication is asynchronous, best-effort, and carries no durability guarantee for in-flight intent.*\n    26\t\n    27\tTwo gaps in the author's mechanism. (a) **The put path also runs `lookup`** (`dht.cljc:214-215`) — iterative `find-closer` round trips, each bounded 500 ms × 3 tries (`node.cljc:236-241`). \"Appends the *target address* to the outbox\" implies peer targets computed at put time, which leaves the blocking lookup on the round's path. The outbox must carry **content addresses** and defer peer resolution to the runner. (b) The runner is unspecified (\"a runner state\"); it must be explicit handle state stopped by `close-fn` (`dht.cljc:255-278`), using the repo's injectable-schedule pump precedent (`dao.jing.md:601-608`) or the caller-stepped cadence (`dao.stream.remote.md` §2.4 Drain).\n    28\t\n    29\t**\"Queues or drops\" with empty bootstrap (weak point 2) — unresolved, and my ruling is: neither, in local-only mode there is no queue.** A memory-log never evicts (\"this namespace contains no expression that removes an element\", `memory_log.cljc:7-9`), so queueing for a deliberately-off network grows without bound for a session's life. Network off ⇒ do not enqueue; network on ⇒ enqueue and let the runner drop after bounded attempts, with a counter. Related architectural point: \"local-only DHT\" should be a **solo `IDhtNet`** (no socket, `known-peers []`, `lookup []`), not a UDP node with empty `:bootstrap` — `create-node` binds a socket and starts a daemon receiver thread regardless (`node.cljc:308-332`). That is what makes owner policy (2) real rather than nominal.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Slice 3 (non-JVM peers) — gap real, shape wrong.** `dao.stream.remote.md:386-387` already names the host primitives (clj `DatagramSocket`, Node `dgram`, cljd `RawDatagramSocket`). Under the slice-1 ruling, slice 3 collapses to two thin host seams feeding `receive!`; the author's three independent peers would triple the wire code and fork the codec story. Browser deferral: agree, and note the chosen transport is what makes the browser path a channel swap later.\n    32\t\n    33\t**Slice 4 (hardening) — required, under-sequenced, under-specified (weak point 4).** Because slice 1 creates the amplification vector, the cookie/rate-limit *design* is a slice-1 gate; only the implementation may wait, and nothing may be exposed beyond loopback before it lands. The mechanism the author leaves unspecified: a Kademlia/BEP-41-style cookie — the receiver issues, via a prior `:ping` from the datagram's source, `hash(source ip:port, nonce, time-bucket)`; any request whose reply will fragment (or any store burst) must carry a cookie obtained from that same source within the window. Replies already go to the datagram source (`node.cljc:190-195`; `udp.cljc:385`), so this closes blind-spoof amplification. Rate limiting needs bounded memory (adversaries rotate source ports): LRU-capped token buckets plus a per-source partial-reassembly cap. I would add the **dead-peer shortlist** fix here (`dao.jing.dht.md:180-184`) — the epic's own read path (foreign manifest fetch) exercises it, and under partition every missing address can cost k × 1.5 s.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Slice 5 (yin.repl integration) — direction right, three omissions.** (1) Unlisted external dependency: the spec mechanism it extends is the durable-store epic's slice 1–3 work, not yet implemented (ground truth 3). (2) Directory lock: a `dht:<dir>` store's local half is `dao.jing.file` in that dir, so the exclusive lock (durable findings §3) still applies — but two REPL processes sharing one DHT must use *different* dirs and bootstrap to each other; say so, or \"DHT = shared storage\" invites two processes on one dir and a corrupted file log. (3) Local-only = solo net (above). What is published and the read path are already correct in outline: the indexer already drains its intake through a generic `content-store` (`yin/repl/index.cljc:148-160,218-229`), and `get`'s verify-and-cache is `dht.cljc:227-252`.\n    36\t\n    37\t**Root discovery (author item 5 / weak point 6) — conclusion right, substance missing, and it does not belong in this epic.** \"A `dao.stream` of root facts over `dao.stream.remote` rendezvous\" is a label, not a design: naming is unimplemented (discovery is proposal-only), writer identity is unauthenticated until ShiBi (peer names are self-certifying but nothing verifies them, `dao.stream.remote.md:503-506`) — unsigned root facts are spam-able with valid-but-foreign manifests, and content addressing gives integrity, not *correct selection*. Retention and reader pinning are likewise open. Crucially, this epic's stated goal does not need it: `<dir>/HEAD` (durable findings §2) *is* root discovery for the single-owner persistent store. Rule: agree it stays outside the DHT; it is a separate epic blocked on discovery + ShiBi, and slice 5 must not wait on it.\n    38\t\n    39\t## B. The three owner-policy questions\n    40\t\n    41\t**(1) Default stays `mem`; `dht:` explicit — agree.** A default `dht:` would bind sockets and emit traffic from a bare REPL start (least surprise), the DHT has no GC/pinning so a default would grow unbounded caches (`dao.jing.dht.md:172-174`), and tests stay deterministic. Recommendation: once the durable epic lands, document three postures — `mem` (default), `file:<dir>` (recommended durable), `dht:<dir>` (networked opt-in).\n    42\t\n    43\t**(2) Network OFF by default even with `dht:` — agree, concretized.** `dht:<dir>` with no configured peers = solo net: no socket, no receiver thread, no outbox appends. \"Network on\" is the explicit act of naming bootstrap peers — and when a socket actually opens, the REPL banner should announce the bound host:port (a listening socket is a security-relevant side effect the user should see; note the current default bind is loopback, `node.cljc:308`). Never queue replication intent while offline (unbounded growth, above).\n    44\t\n    45\t**(3) No sensitive REPL data on the open DHT until ShiBi — agree, and enforce it structurally, not by documentation.** Define \"sensitive\" concretely: *everything the indexer publishes is the user's evaluated source code plus provenance* (session token, root, round ride the metadata entity, `yin/repl/index.cljc:111-115`; `yin.repl.dao.space-index.md:45-51`). Publication is **irreversible** — no deletes by design (`dao.jing.dht.md:13`), replication lands content on k strangers' disks, the network is world-readable, and content addressing delivers integrity, never confidentiality (`dao.stream.remote.md` §1, §7). Enforcement: with bootstrap but no publish opt-in, the store is fetch/cache/serve-only; *originating* publication of locally indexed content requires a second explicit flag (e.g. `--dht-publish`). A documentation-only rule will not survive an eager user. (If the owner insists on one flag, then bootstrap ⇒ full participation must at minimum print exactly what is about to be shared and to whom it will replicate.)\n    46\t\n    47\t## C. Decide before implementation vs per slice\n    48\t\n    49\t**Before any code:** (1) the transport fork — `dao.stream.udp`-based `IDhtNet` vs raw per-host peers — it determines slices 1, 3, and the browser path, and the author buried it as incidental; (2) the ack contract sentence (local-verdict ack; non-durable, re-derivable replication intent); (3) local-only semantics (solo net; no enqueue offline); (4) exposure posture (loopback-only until hardening; the amplification budget named at slice-1 design time); (5) codec freeze (the CBOR clean break is only free before a fleet exists).\n    50\t**Per slice:** cookie construction and rate parameters; runner mechanics and schedule injection; `dht:` spec syntax and flag names; where the solo-net constructor lives; doc updates.\n    51\t\n    52\t## D. My slice plan\n    53\t\n    54\tOrdered; none touches `yin/repl/*` before S5.\n    55\t\n    56\t- **S1 — Transport + CBOR + fragmentation (JVM).** `IDhtNet` over a `dao.stream.udp` port; `DatagramSocket` seam; one CBOR value per reassembled datagram; rpc id compatible with the envelope's `:dao.stream.remote/id`; Transit removed from `node.cljc`. *AC:* two- and three-node JVM put/get of segments > 1200 B and > 64 KiB (oversize refused `:dao.stream.remote/oversize`, never torn); replies to the datagram source only; existing `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc` + `dht/node_test.cljc` green; `dao.jing.dht.md` fixed incl. line 19, and `dao.jing.cbor.md:469`'s DHT-envelope sentence updated.\n    57\t- **S2 — Async replication outbox.** Explicit `{:outbox memory-log :runner …}` in the handle; put returns after the local verdict + enqueue of the *content address*; runner owns lookup + `store-content!`; injectable schedule; stopped by `close-fn`; counters queued/sent/refused/dropped. *AC:* with all peers blackholed, put returns bounded (< ~50 ms, no executor occupancy); late peers eventually receive content; close leaves no pump/thread; the contract documented.\n    58\t- **S3 — Host seams (CLJD `dart:io`, Node `dgram`).** *AC:* clj↔node and clj↔cljd put/get proofs of a multi-fragment segment, mirroring the existing cross-host socket proofs; a > 1200 B blob round-trips off-JVM.\n    59\t- **S4 — Hardening + shortlist pruning.** Cookie challenge for fragment-bearing replies and store bursts; LRU-capped per-source token buckets; per-source reassembly cap; dead-peer replacement. *AC:* an unverified source that would elicit a fragmenting reply gets a cookie, never datagrams; an over-limit peer is refused with bounded memory; a dead shortlist member is replaced by the next-nearest reachable peer in bounded rounds.\n    60\t- **S5 — yin.repl integration (externally gated on the durable-store epic).** `dht:<dir>` spec; solo net when no peers; dir lock via the local file store; HEAD unchanged; bootstrap ⇒ fetch/serve; publish opt-in per policy (3). *AC:* local-only restart sequence identical to the durable findings §5 (evaluate, stop, restart, `q` sees old facts, `t`/provenance continue); two processes on loopback with separate dirs — B's `q` resolves a manifest A published; default start opens no socket (no listener, no receiver thread — assertable).\n    61\t- **Deferred to separate epics:** root-facts stream (blocked on discovery + ShiBi), browser/ws-channel `IDhtNet`, GC/pinning/restart re-enqueue sweep, NAT traversal (`dao.stream.remote.md` §4).\n    62\t\n    63\t**Concurrency:** S1 → {S2, S3} → S4 → S5. The author's \"slices 1–4 can run concurrently\" is incorrect: S2 rewrites `make-put` while S1 rewrites the node's wire and receiver loop (same files); S3 needs S1's port abstraction; S4 needs S1's fragmenting replies and overlaps S3 in the receiver path. Only the \"nothing touches `yin/repl/*` before the integration slice\" half of the claim stands.\n    64\t\n    65\t## Where I DISAGREE with the author, explicitly\n    66\t\n    67\t1. **Outbox ack contract** — \"durably stored locally and queued\" is contradictory in a `memory-log`; fix the contract wording, not the medium.\n    68\t2. **Slice 1's home** — building fragmentation inside `dao.jing.dht.node` duplicates `dao.stream.udp`; the right shape is `IDhtNet` over a `dao.stream.udp` port.\n    69\t3. **\"Slices 1–4 run concurrently\"** — they cannot (shared files, real dependencies S1→S2/S3→S4).\n    70\t4. **Hardening as an unconstrained late slice** — its design gates slice 1, because fragmentation is what creates the ~60× amplification vector.\n    71\t5. **Root-facts item** — a label, not a design, and unnecessary for this epic (HEAD already covers its goal); it should be removed from this epic's slice list.\n    72\t6. **Missed defect** — the synchronous `lookup` on the put path: an outbox of precomputed peer targets still blocks the REPL round; enqueue addresses.\n    73\t7. **Missed dependency** — slice 5 extends a startup spec mechanism that does not exist yet (durable-store epic).\n    74\t8. **\"Local-only DHT\" as empty bootstrap** — nominally off but still binds a socket and spawns a receiver thread; use a solo net.\n    75\t\n    76\tWhere I agree and add nothing: the five blockers and their evidence; per-fragment hashes unnecessary; local-first durable insert + best-effort replication; root discovery outside the DHT; `mem` default; network-off default; no-REPL-data policy; browser deferral via a remote channel.\n    77\t\n    78\t---\n    79\t\n    80\tA note on process: this ran under plan mode, so beyond reading I wrote only the sanctioned plan file (`/Users/sto/.claude-glm/plans/read-collab-1790750376000-architect-dao-polymorphic-hippo.md`), which records this same deliverable outline. The task itself is complete with this response — it is read-only and this text is the deliverable; the orchestrator captures it into `collab/`. Nothing was edited in the repository.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'create-content|dao.jing.content|HEAD|discovery|not implemented|Not implemented|default-hash-algorithm|canonical|DHT envelope' docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | head -95; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '250,267p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc | sed -n '1,67p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc | sed -n '203,224p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'create-content|dao.jing.content|HEAD|discovery|not implemented|Not implemented|default-hash-algorithm|canonical|DHT envelope' docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md | head -95; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc | sed -n '250,267p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht/node.cljc | sed -n '1,67p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc | sed -n '203,224p'\"","aggregated_output":"collab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:16:Use `<dir>/content.jing` for `dao.jing.file` and a separate `<dir>/HEAD` for the latest published manifest address. The file store persists and validates content, but currently has no mutable root pointer; `create-content-file` takes a file path, not a directory ([file.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:405)). After a round drains all blobs and reads its manifest back, write a versioned HEAD record containing the manifest address to a temporary file in the same directory, sync it, atomically rename it over HEAD, and sync the directory where the host supports that operation. Only then report the round as durably published. This extends the current publish sequence in [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:234).\ncollab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:18:On open, absent HEAD means an empty index. A malformed HEAD, missing manifest, invalid manifest, or unreadable index node is a startup error; never silently start an empty index. Validate with `read-manifest` and a full `read-datoms` traversal ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:306), [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:332)). An unreferenced blob after a crash is harmless. A crash before HEAD replacement retains the previous published snapshot.\ncollab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:26:Refuse a second REPL process using the same durable directory. Acquire an exclusive directory lock before opening the content file or reading HEAD, hold it through shutdown, and fail startup with the directory named if acquisition fails. A single owner avoids competing HEAD updates and concurrent append/recovery against a backend whose write lock protects one handle, rather than separate processes ([file.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:405)).\ncollab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:34:Touch [main.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc:48), [driver.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:75), [repl.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:721), and [index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc:136); add a small REPL owned durable directory component for HEAD and locking. `dao.jing.file` remains the blob backend. Update [yin.repl.dao.space-index.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md:1) with the startup and recovery contract.\ncollab/1790709703000-architect-repl-durable-index-store-startup.gpt-6-sol.findings.md:36:Implement in three slices: (1) CLI/spec parsing and store lifecycle; (2) HEAD, lock, and recovery; (3) indexer rehydration and reset continuity. Acceptance tests should cover the memory default, invalid specs and paths, all supported hosts, second-process refusal, missing and corrupt HEAD, interrupted publication on either side of HEAD replacement, and the essential restart sequence: evaluate code, stop, restart against the same directory, require `dao.space.query`, verify `q` sees the old facts, evaluate more code, and verify both old and new facts with increasing `t` and distinct session provenance. Also verify `(reset)` retains those facts in durable mode.\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing.content\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:16:   text or canonical CBOR alike.\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:21:   client half is dao.jing.content.step; the JVM blocking driver is\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:22:   dao.jing.content.driver; the callback facade is\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:23:   dao.jing.content.async. Every put passes through the one ingress\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:24:   canonicality check, dao.jing/accept-bytes!.\"\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:11:is supplied to its observer and is not a discovery registry either.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:23:internet-scale discovery mechanism. It hits three ceilings at scale:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:27:2. **Query composition presupposes discovery.** Constructing a descriptor\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:34:So discovery must become what everything else in this architecture becomes:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:116:produce a reader's explicit source pool: discovery becomes a query over single-writer\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:119:### 4. Rendezvous topics (discovery by interest)\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:162:  discovery, no ledger.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:163:- **Paying for discovery service** — buying an indexer's query answers.\ndocs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:164:  **Accepted.** That is pay-for-truth applied to discovery, already the doctrine.\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc:8:   canonical profile, rides one datagram, at most `max-datagram` bytes,\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:7:   effects are explicit functions over canonical CBOR bytes: put takes an\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:8:   address and its canonical payload bytes and answers :inserted or\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:24:   globals, registration, or discovery,